## Deliberate no-ops, verified rather than assumed

- **`docs/plans/dogfood/README.md:67`** — "Audit whether closed beads have their
  run refs, gate records, and pushed refs." That is precisely (a), (b) and (d),
  so the shrink made this line MORE accurate, not less.
- **`scripts/dogfood-completeness-allow.txt` header** — says entries are exempt
  from the run-ref/gates checks and are REPORTED. Both still true, and the
  second only because the plan gate stopped me dropping the bypass report while
  keeping the file that promises it.
- **Dated entries in `retro-ledger.md`** recording what the audit enforced at the
  time. A ledger entry describing a past state is not a stale pointer, and
  rewriting one to match the present is the opposite of what a ledger is for.

## The rule this bead had to learn twice

"This file is historical" is a judgement about a FILE, and a file is not
uniformly one thing. `retro-ledger.md` is mostly dated history with a
prescriptive convention block in the middle of it. Classifying by filename is
how the block survived the first sweep.

The distinction that actually holds is per-STATEMENT: does this sentence tell a
reader what is true NOW, or record what was true THEN? The ledger's own note now
says that explicitly, so the next person sweeping it has the rule rather than
having to rediscover it.

And the rule is not enough on its own, because I wrote it down and then broke it
again one round later in `docs/retro.md`. What actually caught both rounds was a
seat reading the prose. `make docs-check` and `make docs-reality` are green
throughout and neither reads English — there is no mechanical check in this repo
for "this sentence promises a guarantee that no longer exists", which is the
single most common defect this whole epic has produced.

**`docs/plans/dogfood/retro-capture.md:14`** — read as a live mandate
("Cadence retros **require** a `--meta-file` sidecar … every capture **must**
include"). Now says it is a convention nothing verifies, and names the consumer
that still reads the sidecars.

**`docs/plans/dogfood/supervisor-runbook.md:17`** — a planning note saying "the
audit shrinks to the two checks that caught real misses". The shipped audit keeps
three hard checks plus a warning, because the plan gate overturned the premise
that two of them were redundant. It under-claimed rather than over-claimed, but
it mis-described what landed.

## New documentation

None. This bead removes checks; the model that replaces them was documented by
bead `.2`. What it adds instead is a 40-line header on the audit itself
explaining why checks (a) and (b) survive — the record of a premise the plan gate
overturned, kept where the next person to shrink this file will read it.

